<%#
  TWO INTROS, because the two recipients are in different positions. A Redmine user is a
  colleague who can open the report's project; an allowlisted external address belongs to
  somebody with no account here, so the mail says which installation it came from and does
  not invite them to a page they cannot reach.
%>
<% if @external %>
<%= l(:text_reporter_adhoc_mail_intro_external, name: @template_name, user: @requester.name, site: Setting.app_title) %>
<% else %>
<%= l(:text_reporter_adhoc_mail_intro, name: @template_name, user: @requester.name) %>
<% end %>

<%#
  FR-47 — "shared output is labelled with the identity it was rendered as", and on this
  path that identity is the REQUESTER rather than the recipient. It matters here for the
  same reason it matters on the scheduled path and for one more: an ad-hoc report is a
  colleague handing you their view of the data, so a recipient with narrower access would
  otherwise read numbers they could not have produced themselves and assume they could.

  It is also the sentence that makes `Reply-To` make sense: the person named here is the
  person a reply reaches.
%>
<%= l(:text_reporter_adhoc_mail_rendered_as, user: @requester.name) %>
<% if @project %>
<%= l(:field_project) %>: <%= @project.name %>
<% end %>
<%= l(:label_reporter_report_diagnostic_correlation_id) %>: <%= @correlation_id %>
